'''''Verbena canadensis''''' (syn. ''Glandularia canadensis''), commonly known as '''rose mock vervain''',<ref name="usda">{{cite web |date=n.d. |title=Glandularia canadensis (L.) Nutt. |url=https://plants.usda.gov/plant-profile/GLCA2 |website=plants.usda.gov |publisher=[[Natural Resources Conservation Service]] |access-date=17 December 2024}}</ref> '''rose verbena''',<ref name="MO">{{cite book |last1=Denison |first1=Edgar |title=Missouri Wildflowers |date=2017 |publisher=Conservation Commission of the State of Missouri |isbn=978-1-887247-59-7 |pages=55 |edition=Sixth}}</ref> '''clump verbena'''<ref name="NC"/> or '''rose vervain'''<ref name=":0">{{Cite web|date=n.d.|title=Glandularia canadensis - Plant Finder|url=https://www.missouribotanicalgarden.org/PlantFinder/PlantFinderDetails.aspx?kempercode=f204|access-date=4 December 2021|website=[[Missouri Botanical Garden]]|archive-date=4 December 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20211204222625/https://plants.ces.ncsu.edu/plants/verbena-canadensis/|url-status=live}}</ref> is a [[perennial]] [[herbaceous]] [[flowering plant]] in the verbena family ([[Verbenaceae]]) with showy pink to purple flowers. It is native to the eastern and south-central areas of the United States.<ref name="262956-2" >{{cite POWO |id=262956-2 |title=''Verbena canadensis'' (L.) Britton |access-date=4 December 2021 }}</ref> This species is widely cultivated as an ornamental, and naturalized populations have been established outside its native range, such as in the northeastern U.S.<ref>{{cite web |date=n.d. |title=Glandularia canadensis |url=https://gobotany.nativeplanttrust.org/species/glandularia/canadensis/ |website=[[New England Wild Flower Society]] |access-date=18 December 2024 |archive-date=8 July 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240708032525/https://gobotany.nativeplanttrust.org/species/glandularia/canadensis/ |url-status=live }}</ref><ref name=Missouri>{{cite book |last=Yatskievych |first=George |date=2013 |title=Flora of Missouri, Volume 3 |publisher=Missouri Botanical Garden Press |pages=1208 }}</ref>

==Description==
''V. canadensis'' is a perennial herb<ref name=":1">{{Cite web|date=n.d.|title=Glandularia canadensis|url=https://www.wildflower.org/plants/result.php?id_plant=glca2|access-date=4 December 2021|website=[[Lady Bird Johnson Wildflower Center]]|archive-date=4 December 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20211204164946/https://www.wildflower.org/plants/result.php?id_plant=glca2|url-status=live}}</ref> that grows low to the ground, typically to about {{convert|1|ft|cm|0|}} high. Roots will form where stems touch the ground, and over time the plant will spread to form a low mound.<ref>{{cite web |last=ANPS Webmaster |title=Know Your Natives – Rose Vervain |url=https://anps.org/2018/02/13/know-your-natives-rose-vervain/ |website=Arkansas Native Plant Society |language=en |date=13 February 2018 |access-date=18 December 2024 |archive-date=27 March 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230327172015/https://anps.org/2018/02/13/know-your-natives-rose-vervain/ |url-status=live}}</ref> Leaves, up to {{convert |3|in|cm|0|order=flip|}}long, are dark green on the upper surface and lighter green below. Leaves are semi-evergreen, opposite and pinnately-lobed.<ref name="NC">{{Cite web|date=n.d.|title=Verbena canadensis (Clump Verbena, Rosa Verbena, Rose Vervain) North Carolina Extension Gardener Plant Toolbox|url=https://plants.ces.ncsu.edu/plants/verbena-canadensis/|access-date=4 December 2021|website=plants.ces.ncsu.edu|archive-date=4 December 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20211204222625/https://plants.ces.ncsu.edu/plants/verbena-canadensis/|url-status=live}}</ref> Flowers are pink to purple, and consist of a tubular [[corolla (botany)|corolla]] that opens up into 4 or 5 spreading lobes. Flowers bloom in the spring and can persist until fall.<ref name=Missouri/> The plant attracts butterflies, rabbits, and deer.<ref name=":1" />

Often cultivated for its perennial habit in cold-weather climates, selected varieties or hybrids<ref>{{Cite web |title=Verbena 'Snow Flurry' |url=https://plantfinder.mobot.org/PlantFinderDetails.aspx?bt=8&ht=1&isprofile=0&st=2&taxonid=258341 |website=Missouri Botanical Garden Plant Finder}}</ref> of ''V. canadensis'' include "Homestead Purple" (purple flowers), "Snow Flurry" (white flowers), "Carlos" (bicolor flowers; white and lilac), and others.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Stagg |first=Jason |last2=Owings |first2=Allen D. |title=Plants with Potential - 2015, 2016 and 2017 |url=https://www.lsuagcenter.com/articles/page1463768809201 |website=LSU AgCenter}}</ref> Some cold-hardy cultivated varieties of verbena are also derived from the related [[Verbena peruviana|Peruvian verbena]] (''V. peruviana'').<ref>{{Cite web |title=Verbena peruviana |url=https://www.plantdelights.com/products/verbena-peruviana |access-date= |website=Plant Delights Nursery |language=en}}</ref>

==Distribution and habitat==
Its natural habitat is in sunny areas such as glades, forest openings, and on bluffs.<ref name=Missouri/><ref name=IL>{{cite web |date=n.d. |title=Rose Vervain (Glandularia canadensis) |url=http://www.illinoiswildflowers.info/prairie/plantx/rs_vervainx.htm |website=Illinois Wildflowers |access-date=18 December 2024 |archive-date=26 July 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240726145115/https://www.illinoiswildflowers.info/prairie/plantx/rs_vervainx.htm |url-status=live}}</ref> It is tolerant of dry conditions.<ref>{{cite web |date=n.d. |title=Glandularia canadensis |url=https://missouriplants.com/Glandularia_canadensis_page.html |website=Missouri Plants |access-date=18 December 2024 |archive-date=27 May 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240527111951/https://www.missouriplants.com/Glandularia_canadensis_page.html |url-status=live}}</ref> In some areas, the presence of ''Verbena canadensis'' is indicative of high-quality natural communities.<ref name=IL/>

